1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Well, let's kick off this deep dive.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:06,400
Today we will explore an incredibly complex historical dynamic, but we will do it in a

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,720
slightly different way than usual, namely through the rigorous lens of data and political

4
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:11,720
economy.

5
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:14,599
The exact title of our analysis today is just that.

6
00:00:14,599 --> 00:00:16,879
The highways helped Hitler.

7
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:20,600
We will examine, in a purely objective way, a fascinating study, to say the least, published

8
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:25,000
in the American Economic Journal, analyzing a literal mountain of data from the era,

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,760
the authors sought to quantify exactly how major public works

10
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:31,160
can manipulate political support.

11
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:36,280
It is a meticulous academic investigation that will guide us through numbers, maps, and surprising methodologies,

12
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:37,679
in short, let's get started.

13
00:00:37,679 --> 00:042,359
All this research starts from a question that, if we think about it, is truly profound

14
00:00:42,359 --> 00:00:44,799
when analyzing the rise of totalitarian regimes.

15
00:00:44,799 --> 00:00:48,680
How does a dictator, who at the beginning is openly opposed by large segments of the

16
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:52,439
population, manage to obtain almost total consensus within a few months?

17
00:00:52,439 --> 00:00:56,680
It is a crucial question, because power is not consolidated by intimidation

18
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:57,680
or repression alone.

19
00:00:57,679 --> 00:00:59,039
Something more is needed.

20
00:00:59,039 --> 00:01:03,240
A true dictatorship, the kind that also feeds on real popular support, requires

21
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:06,319
literally perfect mechanisms of mass persuasion.

22
00:01:06,319 --> 00:01:11,599
And this brings us to section 3, democracy in danger, because history reminds us

23
00:01:11,599 --> 00:01:15,760
that authoritarian regimes rarely rule by force alone.

24
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:21,200
To truly root power, there is a need to manufacture tangible popular support,

25
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,760
and looking at this timeline, the speed of this transformation is impressive.

26
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:31,320
In March 1933 we have the last semi-free elections, and mind you, at that moment

27
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:35,600
a huge segment of the population was still actively and decisively opposing them.

28
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:39,080
Then in September 1933, something clicks.

29
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:42,040
The first stone of the new highway network is laid.

30
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:46,920
Barely two months later, in November 1933, voting takes place again with only one party

31
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:47,920
allowed.

32
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:51,920
The climate was one of total intimidation, but there were still those who defied the system by spoiling

33
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:52,920
their ballots.

34
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:58,439
The definitive turning point comes in August 1934; a plebiscite grants absolute powers

35
00:01:58,439 --> 00:02:01,840
to the leadership, registering an unprecedented peak in support.

36
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:04,240
But what happened in such a short time?

37
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:08,719
The answer might lie in what we can define as an extremely

38
00:02:08,719 --> 00:02:09,719
visible project.

39
00:02:09,719 --> 00:02:13,520
Our number one suspect is the Autobahn itself.

40
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:17,960
Take a look at this map of the highway network in 1934.

41
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:21,840
Those thick, dark lines are the open construction sites. Do you notice anything strange about this

42
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:22,840
distribution?

43
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:27,080
Work began simultaneously in a myriad of completely disconnected points

44
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:28,080
from one another.

45
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:31,400
From the standpoint of engineering utility, it makes absolutely no sense.

46
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:35,199
It would have made much more sense to finish a functional section entirely first, right?

47
00:02:35,199 --> 00:02:36,199
But instead, no.

48
00:02:36,199 --> 00:02:39,759
From a political economy perspective, it was an incredibly calculated move.

49
00:02:39,759 --> 00:02:43,520
The goal was to maximize the local visibility of the work so that the largest possible number

50
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:47,560
of citizens could literally see with their own eyes the excavators in action

51
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,920
right outside their doorstep.

52
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,800
And here we move on to the section, the proof of the votes.

53
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:56,920
Did all this strategic visibility actually shift the scale's needle,

54
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:57,920
literally?

55
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:01,860
The answer is imprinted in this heat map. It is the result of complex processing

56
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:04,920
of statistics, but the visual message is unmistakable.

57
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:08,880
The darkest red areas, which represent the highest electoral gains in favor

58
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:15,160
of the regime between 1933 and 1934, follow the lines of the new construction sites

59
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:16,160
almost to the millimeter.

60
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:17,920
There is a crazy correlation.

61
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:22,320
Wherever there was digging, local support spiked abnormally.

62
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:27,040
This intuition becomes rigorous statistical proof when looking at this graph.

63
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:31,400
On the horizontal axis, we have the distance in kilometers from the construction sites, while on the

64
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,920
vertical one is the change in votes.

65
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:37,600
Pay attention to those thin lines at the top of the gray columns.

66
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:42,360
Economists call them error bars, and in this case, they indicate a confidence interval

67
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:44,360
of 95%.

68
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:47,320
This means the data is scientifically bulletproof.

69
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:51,639
As we can see, electoral support made a drastic jump precisely in municipalities

70
00:03:51,639 --> 00:03:54,479
located between zero and 20 kilometers from the works,

71
00:03:54,479 --> 00:03:59,159
only to gradually drop as cities were located further from the

72
00:03:59,159 --> 00:04:00,159
fresh concrete.

73
00:04:00,159 --> 00:04:05,959
At this point, a classic methodological problem arises, which we address in the tenth section,

74
00:04:05,959 --> 00:04:07,680
the route test.

75
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:09,560
The legitimate doubt is this.

76
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:13,560
Did the infrastructure generate popularity, or was it the regime that traced the

77
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:16,680
roads to reward regions that were already loyal to it?

78
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:20,879
To solve this enigma, the study brings into play the least-cost paths.

79
00:04:20,879 --> 00:04:25,040
Let's imagine that a software program must calculate the most efficient route between two cities,

80
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:29,840
looking only at the conformation of the terrain, perhaps avoiding steep slopes or large rivers,

81
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:32,160
but totally ignoring politics.

82
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:36,220
Economists use these very virtual and purely geographical routes to isolate

83
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:40,920
the causal effect, stripping the data of any suspicion of political favoritism.

84
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:45,840
Look at how spectacular this overlap is: the theoretical least-cost routes, the thick gray

85
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:50,880
ones, match incredibly well with the sections of highway actually built, indicated

86
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:51,880
in black.

87
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:55,080
This clear correspondence proves an essential fact.

88
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:59,320
The highways followed a rigid and cold logic of geographical efficiency, not

89
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:01,080
electoral convenience.

90
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:04,960
This allows us to state with certainty that it was indeed the infrastructure that caused

91
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:08,920
a genuine boom in popularity in the areas crossed, and not vice versa.

92
00:05:08,920 --> 00:05:11,800
But what exactly triggered this enthusiasm?

93
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:15,000
We come to the section, propaganda versus economy.

94
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:17,480
What really made people change their minds?

95
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:20,680
Was it real economic relief brought by the construction sites?

96
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:23,480
Or was it a masterful propaganda illusion?

97
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:25,480
The comparison is astounding.

98
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:29,560
In reality, the direct economic benefits of the construction sites were very scarce.

99
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:33,800
At the peak of activity, just over 100,000 workers were employed, which was

100
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:37,560
barely a drop in the ocean compared to the millions of unemployed at the time.

101
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:42,439
Furthermore, the data confirms that unemployment fell uniformly everywhere, throughout

102
00:05:42,439 --> 00:05:46,639
the country, due to a general economic recovery unrelated to the highways.

103
00:05:46,639 --> 00:05:51,360
The effect of the propaganda, however, was massive; it completely distorted reality,

104
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:55,839
convincing everyone that the recovery was solely and uniquely due to those public works.

105
00:05:55,839 --> 00:06:00,159
They knew how to use mass media amplification in a terrifying way.

106
00:06:00,159 --> 00:06:04,959
They organized live radio broadcasts so that the noise of jackhammers

107
00:06:04,959 --> 00:06:07,879
would resound in living rooms across the nation.

108
00:06:07,879 --> 00:06:10,680
The front pages of newspapers talked of nothing else,

109
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:16,079
orchestrating pompous inaugurations even for just a tiny completed kilometer.

110
00:06:16,079 --> 00:06:20,959
And the ultimate proof is that the highest electoral peaks were recorded exactly in the

111
00:06:20,959 --> 00:06:25,519
places where the construction sites coincided with excellent radio signal strength.

112
00:06:25,519 --> 00:06:30,280
They turned a simple strip of asphalt into the tangible symbol of a nation

113
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:32,759
rising from economic stagnation.

114
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:38,199
Which brings us to draw our conclusions in the section, the power of perception.

115
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:42,639
The numbers we analyzed leave us with a clear lesson on how distant

116
00:06:42,639 --> 00:06:45,879
reality can be from its public perception.

117
00:06:45,879 --> 00:06:51,019
The evidence tells us that building highly visible infrastructure projects is a

118
00:06:51,019 --> 00:06:54,959
formidable tool to consolidate autocratic power.

119
00:06:54,959 --> 00:06:56,759
And not because of the work itself.

120
00:06:56,759 --> 00:07:01,399
At the time, there were very few cars in circulation, the logistical benefits were

121
00:07:01,399 --> 00:07:05,719
ridiculous, and the success of the Autobahn was 100% symbolic.

122
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:11,600
It sent a perfect message, creating the illusion of a highly competent, energetic government,

123
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:16,720
capable of resolving, with the force of concrete, all the instability that had characterized

124
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:17,880
the previous era.

125
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:22,480
And here is a final reflection that should set off an alarm bell

126
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:23,480
today.

127
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:27,560
Studying these dynamics of the past is the only real way to keep our eyes open

128
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:28,560
on the present.

129
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:33,840
How might giant and highly visible contemporary infrastructure projects—perhaps

130
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:39,840
immense stadiums in the desert, new pharaonic capitals, or colossal dams—mask

131
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:44,640
the real intentions and lack of freedom of today's authoritarian regimes?

132
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:48,840
Understanding the math behind historical events gives us a powerful weapon to read

133
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:51,840
our present with a much more critical spirit.

134
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:56,160
We hope this analysis has provided interesting insights, and we'll see you in the next

135
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:56,960
deep dive.